1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 (2. d4 {There is a big difference if we start with d4 on the second move:} exd4 3. Qxd4 Nc6 4. Qd3 {Without Nc3-Nf6 included Black has:} d5 $1 {[%csl Rb1,Bd5]} 5. Qxd5 (5. exd5 Nb4 6. Qb3 Qxd5 $36 7. Qxd5 Nxd5 $36) 5... Qxd5 6. exd5 Nb4 7. Bb5+ Bd7 8. Bxd7+ Kxd7 9. Kd1 Nxd5 $36 {[%csl Bd5] White's position is not desirable.}) 2... Nf6 {This is actually a very challenging move order} (2... Nc6 3. f4 {We don't play it here, so we also don't do it against Nf6.} (3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 {is our idea instead of 3.f4.})) 3. d4 {This idea doesn't give White an advantage, but it's tricky, and Black should know how to handle it.} (3. Bc4 Nxe4 {[%csl Be4]} 4. Qh5 (4. Nxe4 $2 d5 $17) 4... Nd6 5. Bb3 Be7 $1 {[%csl Be7]} (5... Nc6 $2 6. Nb5 {[%csl Bb5] And White is better.}) 6. f4 {Interesting and rare gambit, but Black has a clear solution:} (6. Nf3 {With the idea to recapture with the knight, but it doesn't offer anything for White.} Nc6 7. Nxe5 g6 $1 8. Nxc6 (8. Qe2 {Runs into:} Nd4 $1 $15 {And Black is even slightly better.}) 8... dxc6 9. Qf3 Nf5 $132 {[%cal Ge8g8,Rd2d4,Gf5d4] Black is at least fine.}) (6. Qxe5 O-O 7. Nf3 Nc6 8. Qf4 Na5 $11 {[%cal Ga5b3,Gf8e8,Ge7f8] This is not too difficult to play with Black.}) 6... g6 $1 7. Qe2 e4 8. d3 exd3 9. Qxd3 O-O 10. h4 Na6 $1 {In the game Black played c5 which was a mistake. This was the correct approach to seize the bishop on b3.} (10... c5 11. Bd5 Nc6 12. h5 Nb4 13. Qh3 g5 14. fxg5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Bxg5 16. Nf3 Bxc1 17. Rxc1 Re8+ 18. Kf1 Kh8 19. h6 Re4 20. Re1 Qg8 21. Qh2 Qg6 22. Rxe4 Nxe4 23. Qe5+ f6 24. Qe7 b6 25. Nf4 Ba6+ 26. Kg1 Qe8 27. Qg7# {1-0 Christiansen,J (2620)-Holm,K (2450) Paleochora op 14th 2021 (4.1)}) 11. h5 Nc5 12. Qh3 g5 $1 $15 {[%csl Bg5] It's worse for White. I am fine with complicated positions, as long as they are objectively fine, but not if they are worse.}) 3... exd4 4. Qxd4 Nc6 5. Qd3 {This is the starting position of this chapter and here Black has three serious options.} Bc5 {Black's most ambitious move, and the engine shows -0.10, but we are happy to face it, as we get a big fight.} (5... Bb4 {The mainline and we are very likely to face this move. But, we are very happy with this.} 6. Bd2 O-O 7. O-O-O Re8 {Is the best for Black.} (7... d6 $6 {Common mistake.} 8. Qg3 $1 {[%cal Gf2f3]} Re8 (8... Ne5 {Also played in some games.} 9. f3 Be6 10. Nge2 $16 {White is just positionally better.}) (8... Bxc3 {Doesn't win material.} 9. Bxc3 $16 {Black cannot take, due to mate on g7.}) 9. f3 Be6 10. Nge2 $16 {[%csl Be2] White is seriously better.}) 8. Nge2 Ne5 (8... d6 {Also here this is inaccurate, as it allows:} 9. Bg5 $1 h6 10. Bxf6 Qxf6 11. Nd5 Qd8 12. Nxb4 Nxb4 13. Qd2 {With a great situation for White.} a5 {Black's best try.} (13... Nxa2+ $4 {Simply loses to:} 14. Kb1 $18 {[%csl Ra2] The knight is gone.}) (13... Rxe4 $2 {Also loses material.} 14. f3 {[%csl Re4]} Rh4 15. g3 {Rc4 is met by Nd4 and:} Nxa2+ 16. Kb1 Ra4 {Also doesn't save the piece.} 17. b3 Ra3 18. Kb2 Ra6 19. Nd4 {Now we get the knight.} Rb6 20. Kxa2 c5 21. Nb5 $18 {Black just doesn't have enough compensation.}) (13... Nc6 {Just gives White a great game.} 14. Nc3 Be6 15. f4 $16 {White is positionally better.}) 14. a3 Na6 {[%cal Ga6c5]} 15. f3 b5 {Best for the engine.} (15... Nc5 {Is easy to meet.} 16. Nf4 $14 {[%cal Gc1b1,Gf4d5,Gg2g4,Gh2h4,Gg4g5] With a very simple kingside attack.}) 16. Nf4 b4 {I will show you how to easily defend this attack:} 17. a4 {[%csl Ba4]} Nc5 18. b3 $14 {[%cal Gh2h4,Gg2g4] With big potential for White on the kingside.}) 9. Qg3 Nh5 $1 (9... d6 $2 {A mistake, as it allows:} 10. Bg5 $16 {With a big advantage for White.}) 10. Qg5 {[%csl Bg5] Important move, forcing the endgame.} (10. Qh3 {Looks logical, but doesn't give an advantage.} g6 11. g4 d5 $1 $13 {It's not worse for White, but very messy and unclear.}) 10... Qxg5 11. Bxg5 c6 {[%cal Rc3d5] Against Nd5.} 12. h3 {[%cal Gg2g4]} Bc5 {[%cal Rg5e3] A logical move against Be3.} (12... h6 {Also logical, but here we have:} 13. Be3 Nf6 14. Bd4 d6 15. Ng3 $14 {[%cal Gf2f4] And this is slightly better for White.}) 13. Nd4 {[%csl Bd4][%cal Gg5e3] But we nevertheless get Be3.} h6 14. Be3 Nf6 15. Nf5 Bxe3+ 16. Nxe3 $14 {[%cal Gf2f3] This endgame is very playable, and even slightly better for White.}) (5... d5 $5 {Probably the most unpleasant line to face. We don't risk anything here, but if Black knows theory then it ends in a draw.} 6. Bf4 (6. exd5 {Of course after:} Nb4 $11 {It's nothing.}) 6... d4 {Leads to a forced draw.} (6... dxe4 {Here we get a playable endgame.} 7. Qxd8+ Nxd8 (7... Kxd8 {Is strange.} 8. O-O-O+ Bd7 9. Nge2 $14 {[%cal Ge2g3] We recover the pawn, and simply play with the better king.}) 8. Bxc7 {There are some games here, but after recovering the pawn we can definitely play this.} Bb4 (8... Ne6 {Has also been played.} 9. Be5 Ng4 10. Bg3 f5 {Looks very concrete.} 11. h3 Nf6 12. O-O-O Bc5 {We reached a new position, and here we can try some ideas. For example:} 13. Nd5 (13. f3 {Also logical, and Black needs to find:} Bd7 14. fxe4 Nxe4 15. Nxe4 fxe4 16. Bc4 $132 {We have a playable endgame, and it's risk free for White.}) 13... Bd7 {Only move for Black to be fine.} 14. Nxf6+ gxf6 15. Ne2 $132 {We definitely reached a playable position.}) 9. O-O-O Nc6 10. Nge2 Bg4 11. h3 {To keep the structure intact.} (11. a3 {Was tried in one game, but it allows Black to ruin our structure.} Bxc3 12. bxc3 O-O 13. h3 {Horvath - Melikhov, 2022.} Be6 $11 {It seems fine for Black.}) 11... Bxc3 12. hxg4 Be5 13. Bxe5 Nxe5 14. f3 exf3 15. gxf3 {[%csl Rb7,Bg2][%cal Gf1g2,Gg4g5,Gf3f4] Here Black should play concretely, otherwise we are just better:} Nxf3 16. Bg2 Ne5 17. Bxb7 Rb8 18. g5 Nfg4 {Black needs to maintain a normal structure.} (18... Rxb7 {Isn't a good trade for Black.} 19. gxf6 gxf6 20. Nf4 $14 {Definitely some chances here, as our structure is much better.}) 19. Bg2 Ne3 20. Rdg1 Nxg2 21. Rxg2 $132 {It's equal, but White definitely has practical chances, as it's easier to advance the pawns. The g5-pawn is very annoying for Black.}) 7. Nb5 Bb4+ 8. c3 dxc3 {Critical.} (8... Ba5 9. O-O-O Qe7 10. Nxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Bb6 {Only move for Black now.} 12. Qa4+ Bd7 13. Qc2 O-O-O 14. Bd3 {It seems like White is just up a pawn, but for the engine it's almost full compensation.} Ng4 15. Bg3 {No games here and with normal moves White could definitely be slightly better. But, Black can go for the concrete:} Bxf2 16. Bxf2 Ba4 {Very much in the engine style, but this is clearly compensation for Black.} 17. b3 Qa3+ 18. Kb1 Bxb3 {Another strong sacrifice, and the line continues with:} 19. axb3 Nxf2 20. Qxf2 Qxb3+ 21. Kc1 Qxc3+ 22. Qc2 Qa3+ 23. Kd2 Rxd3+ {Now Black gets the queen.} 24. Qxd3 Rd8 25. Qxd8+ Kxd8 26. Ne2 $13 {And for the engine it's of course "0.00" but over the board anything can happen.}) 9. bxc3 (9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. Bxc7+ Ke7 11. bxc3 Bc5 12. e5 Ne8 (12... Ne4 13. f3 a6 {Forced.} (13... Nf2 $2 14. Bd6+ $18 {White just wins a piece.}) 14. fxe4 axb5 15. Bxb5 $14) 13. O-O-O Bxf2 14. Bd6+ Nxd6 15. exd6+ Kd8 16. Nc7 {We can include this to force the rook to b8.} Rb8 17. Nf3 Bd7 18. Bc4 $132) 9... Ba5 10. Qxd8+ Kxd8 11. O-O-O+ (11. f3 a6 {[%emt 0:00:00] Necessary here, and transposes into 11.0-0-0.}) 11... Bd7 12. f3 {Necessary.} a6 13. Nd4 Nxd4 14. cxd4 {Visually we are doing great, but Black can force a draw with:} Ba4 15. Rd3 Bb5 16. Rd1 Ba4 $11) (5... Be7 6. Bf4 {[%csl Re7]} d6 7. O-O-O O-O 8. h3 $14 {[%cal Gg2g4] White just achieved a great position.}) (5... g6 $2 6. Bg5 Bg7 7. f4 $16) 6. Bf4 d6 {Logical continuation.} (6... O-O {A less accurate move order, as it gives White the chance to play:} 7. O-O-O Ng4 (7... Bxf2 $6 8. e5 $14) 8. Nh3 Nxf2 9. Nxf2 Bxf2 10. e5 $132 Bd4 11. Qg3 $14) 7. O-O-O (7. Qd2 Be6 8. O-O-O Bb4 $1 $36) (7. f3 $2 Nh5 $17 {[%csl Bh5][%cal Gd8h4]}) 7... Bxf2 $1 (7... Ng4 {To take on f2 with the knight.} 8. Nh3 $1 Nxf2 9. Nxf2 Bxf2 10. Qf3 Bd4 (10... Bc5 {½-½ Thorfinnsson,B (2432)-Gretarsson,H (2437) ISL-ch Kopavogur 2021 (2)}) 11. Bb5 {To force Black to take.} Bxc3 12. Qxc3 O-O 13. Bxc6 bxc6 14. e5 d5 15. Be3 $14 {[%csl Rc6,Be3] We have a very good bishop and a lot of potential.} Bd7 (15... Qd7 {Looks normal, but after:} 16. Rhf1 $16 {It's suddenly much better for White (+1 evaluation).}) 16. b3 $14 {[%csl Bb3][%cal Ge3d4,Gh1f1,Gh2h4,Gg2g4] We can play normal moves for now, the position is very pleasant.}) (7... O-O {Very easy for Black.} 8. f3 {We are quite happy to play this move.} Be6 9. Qd2 $1 Nd7 10. Kb1 $14 {[%cal Gg1e2,Gh2h4];0-1 Souleidis,G (2410)-Christiansen,J (2583) Titled Tuesday intern op 20th Sep Late Chess.com INT blitz 2022 (8) Simply very comfortable for White.}) (7... Be6 {Similar to 7...O-O.} 8. f3 Qd7 9. Be3 {We exchange the bishops here.} Bxe3+ 10. Qxe3 a6 {Necessary and now Black manages O-O-O.} (10... O-O-O $6 {Fails to:} 11. e5 $16 {[%csl Ra7] Problems with a7.}) 11. h4 O-O-O 12. Nge2 $14 {[%cal Ge2f4] But now it's a normal position, and White is slightly better.}) 8. Nf3 (8. e5 $6 {Doesn't work, due to:} Nh5 $15 {Black's key idea.}) 8... O-O {The only way that Black can keep the small plus.} (8... Bb6 9. Bg5 (9. Be2 {0-1 Dubov,D (2699)-Nakamura,H (2736) Carlsen Tour Final chess24.com INT rapid 2020 (1.24)}) 9... h6 10. Bh4 g5 (10... O-O 11. Be2 $132 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Gh1f1] There could be some problems with the knight on f6.}) 11. Be1 O-O 12. Kb1 $132 {[%csl Rg5]}) (8... Be6 9. Qe2 $1 {Here we go for the e5 idea.} Bc5 10. e5 Nd7 11. exd6 Bxd6 12. Bxd6 cxd6 13. Rxd6 Qe7 14. Qd2 O-O-O (14... O-O {Runs into:} 15. Bc4 $14 {[%csl Rd7] And Black has problems with d7.}) 15. Qf4 Nc5 16. Rxd8+ Rxd8 17. Bb5 $132) (8... Bg4 9. h3 Bxf3 10. Qxf3 Bd4 11. Bb5 $1 {Forcing Black to take on c3.} Bxc3 12. Qxc3 O-O 13. Rhe1 Nh5 14. Bxc6 (14. Bd2 {0-1 Jaskolka,T (2258)-Bargsten,J (2192) Wroclaw op-A 2024 (8)}) 14... Nxf4 15. Bxb7 Rb8 16. Ba6 $14 {This can potentially be better for White, as the bishop could easily be stronger than the knight.}) 9. Qe2 Bc5 {The best move, and here Black keeps the beloved "minus" evaluation.} (9... Bb6 10. Bg5 {We go for the same critical line as against Bc5.} h6 11. Bh4 Bg4 12. h3 Nd4 13. Qd3 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ne6 {And in the same position with the bishop on c5 we play e5, but here we can play:} (14... g5 {0-1 Sahib,S (2438)-Pranav,V (2609) Titled Tuesday intern op 17th Sep Early Chess.com INT blitz 2024 (5)}) 15. Nd5 {To take on b6.} Ng5 16. Nxb6 axb6 17. Kb1 $132 {[%csl Bf1,Bh4][%cal Gh1g1] "0.00" for the engine, but it's much easier for White with the pair of bishops. I certainly wouldn't be happy here with Black.}) (9... Nh5 $2 10. Bxd6 $1 cxd6 11. Qxf2 $16 {1-0 Amar,E (2343)-Fridman,D (2626) Kragero op 2022 (2)}) 10. Bg5 h6 {Necessary.} (10... Bg4 $6 11. Nd5 $14 {1-0 Klek,H (2294)-Dakic,M (1957) EU-ch (Women) 24th Rhodes 2024 (1)}) 11. Bh4 Bg4 {To prepare Nd4.} (11... Re8 12. Nd5 g5 13. Nxg5 Nxd5 14. Rxd5 Qf6 15. Qd2 Nb4 16. Nf3 Qg6 17. Rxc5 dxc5 18. a3 {It's very easy to go wrong with Black.} a5 $1 {[%cal Rf1d3] It's important not to allow Bd3, and White of course doesn't want to allow Black to have the open a-file.} (18... Nc6 19. Bd3 $16 {[%cal Ge4e5] With e5 next, Black is in serious trouble.} Ne5 $2 {Simply loses.} 20. Nxe5 Rxe5 21. Rf1 $18 {Rf3-Rg3 ideas, and it's game over for Black.}) 19. e5 Bf5 20. Bf6 Nxc2 {This is a forced line:} 21. Bc4 Ne3 $1 22. Be2 Bb1 $1 23. Ne1 {Now the game can end in an amusing repetition.} Bf5 (23... c4 $5 {Black can also keep the game going.} 24. Qxe3 Qxf6 25. Kxb1 Rxe5 26. Qg3+ {We can just repeat.} Rg5 27. Qe3 Re5 28. Qg3+ $13 {Black can repeat, or keep the game going, but it will also be risky for him to play on.}) 24. Nf3 {[%cal Gf3h4]} Bb1 $11 {But, of course finding all these moves over the board is extremely difficult.}) 12. h3 Nd4 13. Qd3 Bxf3 14. gxf3 Ne6 15. e5 (15. Nd5 {This was our move against the 9...Bb6 line, but here after:} Ng5 $15 {[%csl Bc5][%cal Rd5b6] We simply don't have Nxb6.}) 15... dxe5 {Black is forced to take.} 16. Qf5 Be3+ 17. Kb1 Nd4 {Only move.} 18. Qxe5 Re8 {Another only move for Black.} 19. Qh2 c6 {This is the only variation where Black keeps the small minus.} 20. Ne4 g5 {Forced.} 21. Bg3 {Black can play Nxe4 or Qe7, and it's only slightly worse for him, but only:} Nd5 22. Bd3 Qb6 23. Ka1 $13 {[%cal Gh3h4]} *

