1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Bc5 (2... Nc6 3. Bc4 Bc5 4. Qh5 {[%csl Bh5] Is analyzed via the 2...Bc5 move order. Doesn't have an independent value}) 3. Qh5 {[%csl Bh5] To keep it independent, and it's very sound! Only eleven games here, and the engine already shows a small plus for White.} (3. Bc4 {If we just play normally, it's difficult to get something.} Nf6 4. d3 O-O $132 {This position is well known, and Black is very solid.}) 3... Nc6 {Most natural move.} (3... d6 4. Bc4 Qd7 (4... g6 5. Qf3 Nf6 (5... Be6 {Also a logical way to defend f7.} 6. Bxe6 fxe6 7. Na4 $1 {To take the bishop.} Bb6 8. Nxb6 axb6 9. Qc3 $1 (9. d3 Nc6 10. Ne2 Nb4 $132 {This could be annoying.}) 9... Nc6 10. d3 {[%csl Bc3][%cal Rc6b4,Gb2b4] No more problems with Nb4.} Nf6 11. Nf3 $14 {White's position is simply better.}) (5... Qf6 $2 6. Nd5 Qxf3 7. Nxf3 Bb6 8. Ng5 {[%csl Rf7] Pressure on f7.} Nh6 9. f4 $18 {[%cal Gh1f1] Black is just busted.}) 6. d3 {[%cal Gc1g5]} h6 7. Nge2 $14 {And it's slightly better for White.}) (4... Qf6 5. f4 $1 {[%csl Rf2,Bf4][%cal Gc3d5] Defending f2!} Ne7 (5... c6 6. d3 $36) (5... Be6 6. Nd5 Bxd5 7. Bxd5 {[%csl Rb7] Black needs to defend b7.} c6 8. Bb3 exf4 9. Ne2 $36 {[%cal Gh1f1] Rf1 next with a great game.}) (5... Qxf4 $4 6. d4 $18) (5... exf4 6. Nd5 Qd8 7. Nxf4 $14) 6. Nge2 Nbc6 7. d3 Qg6 8. Qxg6 hxg6 9. Na4 $14 {We just seize the pair of bishops, with a great endgame.}) 5. Qg5 {[%csl Cg7]} Qg4 (5... g6 6. f4 $16) 6. Qxg4 Bxg4 7. Nd5 Bb6 8. a4 c6 9. a5 Bd8 10. Ne3 Be6 11. d3 $14 {Black is solid but did not fully equalize. It's a very good outcome for White, especially compared to the Petroff or Spanish}) (3... Qf6 $2 4. Nf3 $18 {[%csl Re5]} d6 5. Nd5 Qd8 6. d4 $1 exd4 7. Bg5 Qd7 8. b4 Bb6 9. Bc4 $18) 4. Bc4 Qf6 (4... g6 5. Qf3 Nf6 (5... Qf6 $2 6. Nd5 Qxf3 7. Nxf3 Bd6 (7... Bb6 8. c3 d6 9. a4 Be6 10. Ng5 $18 {White already has a winning advantage according to the engine. So we see that the lead in development can be a huge factor.}) 8. b3 Nge7 9. Bb2 Nxd5 10. exd5 Nb4 11. O-O-O {It's very simple for White.} f6 12. a3 b5 {Necessary.} 13. Bxb5 Nxd5 14. Rhe1 $16 {[%cal Gd2d4] d4 ideas, and White is just much better.}) 6. Nge2 d6 7. d3 (7. h3 {Also a good option if you don't want to allow Bg4.} Be6 {But h3 might not be so useful against Be6.} 8. d3 Nd7 9. Nd5 Nb4 10. Bb3 c6 11. Nxb4 Bxb4+ 12. c3 Bxb3 13. axb3 Bc5 14. h4 $14) 7... Bg4 (7... Be6 $4 8. Bg5 $18) 8. Qg3 Bxe2 (8... h6 {Against Bg5, but it allows:} 9. a3 $14 {[%cal Gc1d2,Ge1c1] To secure the light-squared bishop, and White is simply better.}) (8... Qd7 $6 9. Qh4 $16 {1-0 Iljiushenok,I (2554)-Sarana,A (2649) RUS-ch Rapid Sochi 2021 (7)}) (8... Na5 $2 9. Bg5 $18) 9. Nxe2 Na5 10. Qf3 {We have a nasty trick here.} h6 {Black will understand that we want Bg5.} (10... Nxc4 $2 11. Bg5 $18) 11. Bb3 Nxb3 12. axb3 Qe7 13. Bd2 $14 {[%cal Gb3b4]}) (4... Qe7 $6 {Simply a bad way to defend f7, as it allows:} 5. Nd5 {[%csl Bd5]} Nf6 6. Nxe7 Nxh5 7. Nxc8 Rxc8 8. c3 $16 {[%csl Bc1,Bc4] And it's just a great endgame for White, with the pair of bishops.}) 5. Nf3 (5. f4 $5 {This was strong in the 3...d6 line, but here it's not working so well.} g6 6. Qe2 Nd4 {This is the reason why we don't play f4 in this line, although objectively it's still fine.} 7. Qd3 c6 8. b4 {To prepare Bb2.} Bb6 9. fxe5 Qxe5 10. Bb2 d5 11. Bb3 Bf5 $13) 5... g6 (5... Nge7 6. d3 d6 (6... Qg6 7. Qxg6 hxg6 8. Nb5 {But it's always interesting to play for White.} Bb6 9. Be3 Ba5+ 10. Ke2 {[%cal Gc2c3,Gb2b4]} f6 (10... a6 $6 11. a4 $1 axb5 12. axb5 $16 {Once the knight moves we have b4, regaining the piece.}) 11. a4 Kd8 12. d4 $14) 7. Be3 Bxe3 8. fxe3 Be6 {Black also goes for the bishop trade.} 9. Bxe6 Qxe6 10. O-O-O Qg6 {Black was unable to castle long due to Ng5, so this is the best.} 11. Qh4 {We can keep the queens on now.} O-O-O 12. g4 $36 (12. d4 exd4 13. exd4 d5 14. exd5 Nxd5 15. Nxd5 Rxd5 16. Rd2 Re8 17. Re1 $36 {It's very close to equality, but it's much more comfortable to play for White.})) (5... Qg6 6. Qxg6 hxg6 7. Nd5 Bd6 (7... Bb6 8. a4 $16) 8. O-O Nge7 9. Re1 $14) 6. Qg5 Nd4 (6... h6 7. Qg3 {And here we don't need to exchange anymore.} Nge7 8. Nb5 {To play for d4.} Bb6 9. d4 d6 (9... exd4 $2 10. Nxc7+ $16) 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. Be3 a6 12. Nc3 Bxe3 13. fxe3 $14 {We have the open f-file here, so O-O makes sense, and Nd5 is also an idea. White could be slightly better here.}) (6... Qxg5 $2 7. Nxg5 Nd8 8. f4 exf4 9. d4 Bxd4 10. Nd5 $16 {A romantic way to play the game and White is much better.}) 7. Qxf6 {We have to take now.} Nxf6 8. Nxd4 exd4 (8... Bxd4 {Is less precise.} 9. d3 $14 {With the pawn on g7 it would be equal, but it's on g6, so Black has long-term weaknesses.}) 9. Nb5 Bb6 10. d3 {We don't have an advantage but it's playable and more comfortable.} a6 11. Bg5 {We can exploit the weaknesses of the pawn being on g6.} Nh5 12. Na3 {[%cal Gc2c3]} Ba5+ 13. Bd2 {We need to trade here to realize the c3 plan.} Bxd2+ 14. Kxd2 b5 15. Bb3 Bb7 16. c3 dxc3+ 17. bxc3 $132 {[%cal Gf2f3,Ga3c2,Ga2a4] This is the best that Black can achieve, so we can safely say that the Qh5 idea is sound and interesting. We don't have an advantage here, but practical chances, and it's more pleasant to play.} *

