1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Na5 5. Bb3 (5. Nge2 Bc5 6. O-O O-O 7. Ng3 Nxc4 8. dxc4 d6 $132 {We get this structure, and it's just a balanced game.}) 5... Nxb3 (5... Bc5 6. f4 {If Black takes on b3, it transposes into the Bc5 lines.} d6 {An independent option that we must check.} 7. Qf3 Ng4 {Perhaps this is Black's best option.} (7... Qe7 $6 8. h3 {[%cal Gg1e2] White had the chance to stop any Bg4 ideas, and it's difficult for Black.} (8. f5 {0-1 Robson,R (2689)-Nakamura,H (2802) American Cup Blitz Saint Louis 2025 (6)}) 8... Nxb3 9. axb3 c6 10. Nge2 O-O {Black avoids allowing Bxf4, but now:} (10... exf4 11. Bxf4 $16 {In one tempo. So this is great for White.}) 11. f5 $16 {And also here it's seriously better for White.}) 8. Nge2 Qh4+ 9. g3 Qh3 10. Nd5 {This looks very interesting, and I analyzed it deeper:} Bf2+ 11. Kd2 Bb6 12. Ba4+ Nc6 {Necessary.} (12... c6 $2 13. b4 $18 {Black loses a piece.}) 13. f5 Nxh2 {Critical and necessary for Black at some point.} (13... Bd7 {Also possible, but after:} 14. c3 $132 {Nevertheless Black needs to play Nxh2.}) 14. Rxh2 {We can play for full compensation now.} (14. Nxc7+ {Another interesting way to play.} Bxc7 15. Qf2 Ng4 16. Qg1 Bb6 {Necessary, as Black's queen is trapped.} 17. d4 Qxh1 18. Qxh1 exd4 19. b4 $13 {[%cal Gc1b2] For the engine it's "0.00", but in human terms very unclear and interesting to play.}) 14... Qxh2 15. c3 Bd7 16. Kc2 {Black needs to play concretely now:} Qf2 {The easiest for Black is to give back the material.} (16... O-O-O $2 17. Nxb6+ axb6 18. Be3 $18 {Rh1 is a deadly threat, and Black is losing material here.}) 17. Qxf2 Bxf2 18. Nxc7+ Ke7 19. Nxa8 {Still not over, as Black needs to be precise:} Nb4+ (19... Rxa8 {Doesn't equalize.} 20. Bb3 $14 {White is just positionally better.}) 20. cxb4 Bxa4+ 21. Kd2 Rxa8 {And this can still be interesting. For example:} 22. Nc3 Bd7 23. Ke2 Bxg3 24. Be3 {We have compensation for the pawn for sure.} h5 25. Nd5+ Kf8 26. Rh1 h4 {Black needs to support the bishop.} 27. Bg5 Rc8 28. Kd2 $132 {We recapture on h4. The endgame still offers some practical chances for White, but is of course objectively equal.}) (5... Be7 {Also played in some games.} 6. f4 {We make sure to use this idea here.} Nxb3 7. axb3 d6 8. Nf3 Bg4 9. h3 {To force a reaction from the bishop.} Bxf3 10. Qxf3 c6 11. O-O O-O 12. Ne2 $14 (12. Be3 {½-½ Pranav,V (2598)-Aravindh,C (2703) Abu Dhabi op-A 30th 2024 (9)})) (5... Bb4 {Also possible in the 5...Nxb3 move order.} 6. Nf3 Nxb3 (6... d6 {It's also possible to play without d5 for Black.} 7. O-O O-O 8. Ne2 Nxb3 {Sooner or later Black takes the bishop.} 9. axb3 d5 {Seems like the most interesting move for Black.} (9... h6 {Also logical, to stop any Bg5 ideas.} 10. c3 Bc5 11. Ng3 {We will prepare the d4 push now.} c6 12. Re1 Be6 13. d4 {Taking the center.} Bb6 14. Qc2 Re8 15. h3 $132 {With a playable and interesting position.}) 10. exd5 Qxd5 11. Nc3 (11. Bg5 $132 {Is another possibility, to see how Black reacts.}) 11... Qd6 12. Bd2 {With the idea of Nb5.} Re8 13. Nb5 Qc5 14. Bxb4 Qxb5 15. Bc3 $132 {[%cal Ga1a5] With a playable and interesting position.}) 7. axb3 {This position can also be reached via the 5...Nxb3 move order.} d5 (7... d6 8. O-O O-O 9. Ne2 {Transposes into the 6...d6 move order.} (9. Bg5 c6 10. Kh1 Re8 11. h3 h6 12. Bh4 Qe7 $11 {It seems very easy to play for Black.})) 8. O-O O-O {The best for Black, and here we can see the game Dubov - Ding:} (8... dxe4 9. Nxe5 Bxc3 10. bxc3 O-O 11. Re1 exd3 12. Nxd3 $14 {[%csl Bb2][%cal Gc3c4,Gc1b2,Gb2f6] And we will have a strong bishop on b2. So this can be slightly better for White.}) 9. Bg5 Bxc3 10. bxc3 dxe4 11. Nxe5 h6 (11... exd3 {1-0 Pranav,V (2587)-Yakubboev,N (2665) Sharjah Masters 7th 2024 (5)}) 12. Bxf6 (12. Bh4 exd3 (12... Bf5 $2 13. Re1 (13. Ra5 g5 14. Bg3 Bh7 15. f4 Nd5 16. Qh5 c6 17. Raa1 f6 18. Ng4 Qe8 19. Qxh6 Qg6 20. fxg5 {1-0 Dubov,D (2699)-Ding,L (2791) Lindores Abbey Final 8 chess24.com INT rapid 2020 (2.11)}) 13... exd3 14. cxd3 $16 {With a serious advantage, Black is actually in trouble here.}) 13. cxd3 Re8 14. d4 c5 $11) 12... Qxf6 13. d4 $132 {[%csl Rc8,Be5][%cal Gf2f3] We play for the open f-file next, and perhaps our knight could be stronger than Black's bishop. Definitely interesting to play for White.}) (5... c6 {Has also been tried, but we can just play:} 6. f4 d6 7. Nf3 $36 {With a very good version of the general positions. It's just less dangerous here, as the bishop is not even on c5.}) 6. axb3 d5 {The main move.} (6... d6 7. f4 {[%cal Gg1f3,Ge1g1] And here we have a good version of the Bc5 line. We can see the game Rapport - Niemann now:} exf4 8. Bxf4 Be7 9. Nf3 O-O 10. O-O d5 11. Qe1 {An improvement over Rapport's e5. We can bring the queen to the kingside.} (11. e5 Nh5 $11 {[%cal Gf7f6] Niemann played Ne8 instead, but here he would be fine with f6 next.} (11... Ne8 {0-1 Rapport,R (2802)-Niemann,H (2592) Wch Rapid Almaty 2022 (7)})) 11... c6 12. h3 {[%cal Ge1f2]} a6 13. Kh1 {We can simply improve our position for now.} h6 14. exd5 cxd5 15. Be5 $14) (6... Bc5 {Also logical, and has been played.} 7. f4 d6 8. Qf3 {It's better to prepare f5.} (8. f5 {Immediately allows:} d5 $132 {1-0 Tabatabaei,M (2629)-Triapishko,A (2510) Titled Tuesday intern op 20th Apr Chess.com INT blitz 2021 (6)}) 8... Bg4 {The engine's recommendation.} (8... c6 9. f5 {This was possible here, but White didn't play it.} (9. Nge2 {1-0 Amin,B (2670)-Rakotomaharo,F (2454) Africa-ch Accra 2024 (4)}) 9... d5 10. g4 $14 {We were ready for d5, and this is slightly better for White.}) 9. Qg3 exf4 10. Bxf4 Be6 {It's a difficult but understandable maneuver, Black now has Nh5 ideas.} 11. Bg5 h6 12. Qh4 Rg8 {Best for the engine.} 13. Bxf6 Qxf6 14. Qxf6 gxf6 15. g3 {We can definitely play this endgame. For example:} f5 {Is very natural.} 16. Nge2 fxe4 17. Nxe4 Ke7 18. Nxc5 {We eliminate the pair of bishops now.} dxc5 19. O-O $132 {[%csl Be2,Rf7,Rh6] Of course it's close to full equality, but we have some practical chances and it's risk-free to play for White.}) (6... Bb4 7. Nf3 {And transpose into the 5...Bb4 move order.} (7. Bg5 {I don't like this idea, as it doesn't stop d5.} h6 8. Bh4 d5 9. exd5 {The point is that Black has:} g5 10. Bg3 Nxd5 $15 {White is slightly worse, so Bg5 makes no sense.})) 7. exd5 Nxd5 (7... Bb4 8. Bg5 {This is what modern engines have found in this line.} (8. Bd2 {Is how people usually play here.} Nxd5 $11 {But here Black is fine.}) 8... Qxd5 9. Nf3 Bg4 {Black's idea, but here White has:} (9... e4 {Also a possible try for Black, although it hasn't been played yet.} 10. Bxf6 exf3 11. O-O {An important move to attack the queen.} Bxc3 12. Bxc3 O-O (12... fxg2 $2 13. Re1+ Be6 14. Bxg7 Rg8 15. Be5 $18 {No O-O-O due to a7 hanging, and Black is simply busted.}) 13. Re1 Bh3 (13... fxg2 $2 {Is bad again.} 14. Ra5 $16 {White gets a quick attack, and Black is in serious trouble.}) 14. Ra5 Qc6 15. Qxf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 {But here we are up a pawn, and we definitely can play this endgame. For example:} Rfe8 17. Re3 (17. Re4 f6 18. Rxa7 $132 {Always an idea to win a pawn, and we are not getting mated, as we can simply play f4-f3. It's a playable endgame for White, and we have zero risk.}) 17... f6 18. Rc5 c6 19. b4 a6 {Makes sense, to stop b5.} 20. Rh5 Be6 21. Bd4 Rad8 22. Bb6 Rd5 23. Rh4 (23. Rxd5 cxd5 24. Kf1 $11) 23... h6 24. Kf1 Kf7 25. Ke2 $132 {And we can definitely play this endgame. It's zero risk.}) 10. Ra4 {[%csl Ba4]} Bxf3 11. Qxf3 Qxf3 12. gxf3 Nd5 {To not allow Bxf6.} (12... Bxc3+ 13. bxc3 Nd7 14. Be3 {We are ready to play with f4.} a6 15. Rg4 g6 16. f4 exf4 17. Rxf4 $14 {[%cal Ge1d2] With an interesting structure. I believe that White has good chances here and is slightly better.}) (12... Bd6 13. Bxf6 gxf6 14. f4 {[%csl Re5,Rf6,Rf7][%cal Gf4f5] We want to play f5, to fix Black's structure.} exf4 15. Ke2 $14 {[%cal Ge2f3,Gc3e4,Ge4d6,Ga4f4] White is definitely slightly better and can get a good rook endgame in the future.}) 13. Bd2 Bxc3 14. bxc3 {This is also definitely playable for White.} f6 15. c4 Nb6 16. Rb4 {c5 is a threat.} O-O-O 17. Be3 $132 {[%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5] White has a risk-free and more pleasant endgame.}) 8. Qh5 Nb4 (8... Be7 {Rare, but also good.} 9. Qxe5 Nb4 {Transposes into our mainline 8...Nb4.}) (8... Nxc3 {Is very good for us.} 9. Qxe5+ Be7 10. bxc3 O-O {Of course Black isn't just a pawn down for nothing, but we get a very good endgame:} 11. Qd4 Bf6 12. Qxd8 Bxc3+ {Necessary.} 13. Bd2 Bxd2+ 14. Kxd2 Rxd8 15. b4 $36 {[%csl Bb4,Bd2][%cal Gg1e2,Gc2c3] We have a risk-free and playable endgame. I like the knight in this position, and our structure.}) (8... Qd6 $2 {The third most common, but it's a mistake.} 9. Nf3 $16 {It's not clear how Black will handle the pressure on the e5-pawn.}) 9. Qxe5+ Be7 (9... Qe7 $6 10. Qxe7+ Bxe7 11. Kd1 $16 {1-0 Rusnak,M-Longauer,I SVK-chT3A 0102 Slovakia 2001 (1)}) 10. Kd1 {[%csl Bd1] We protect the pawn with the king. Transposes into our mainline 8...Nb4.} (10. Qe2 $6 O-O 11. Be3 Re8 12. Nf3 Bg4 (12... b6 {0-1 Nepomniachtchi,I (2771)-Carlsen,M (2839) Chess.com Speed Chess Chess.com INT blitz 2023 (2.20)}) 13. O-O Bxf3 $15 {Both recaptures are unpleasant now.}) 10... O-O 11. Nf3 {It's a fresh position with only eight games (mostly correspondence), and here three moves have been tried:} Be6 (11... Re8 $6 {The most common move, but it's a mistake.} 12. Qd4 {[%csl Rc8][%cal Rd8c8] The queen trade is more or less forced.} Qxd4 (12... Bd7 {Keeping the queens allows:} 13. Rxa7 $16 {And here White has a real advantage.}) 13. Nxd4 $16 {In the endgame the extra pawn definitely grants an advantage.}) 12. Bd2 Re8 13. Re1 (13. Nd4 Bg4+ 14. f3 Bf6 {But this line looks a bit shaky.} 15. Qf4 Bd7 16. Ndb5 {To attack the Black knight.} a6 17. Qxb4 axb5 18. Rxa8 Qxa8 19. Qa3 b4 {A very good move for Black.} 20. Qxb4 Qa1+ 21. Bc1 Bg5 22. Na2 $13) (13. Qd4 Qc8 {[%csl Bc8]} 14. Re1 {Was necessary now.} (14. Rxa7 $2 c5 15. Rxa8 Qxa8 16. Qf4 Bf6 17. Ne4 Qa1+ 18. Bc1 Bxb2 19. Ke2 f5 $19 {½-½ Theodorou,N (2611)-Vetokhin,S (2558) Sharjah Masters-A 8th 2025 (8)}) 14... a5 $132) 13... c5 {[%csl Bc5][%cal Re5d4] Black's best move is to stop Qd4.} (13... Qd7 14. Qd4 Qc8 {This is just a better version than the Theodorou game, as we have a full extra tempo more with Re1.} 15. Nb5 c5 16. Qf4 Qd7 {Necessary.} 17. Bxb4 cxb4 18. Nbd4 Bd6 19. Qg5 $14 {We can simplify a bit with Nxe6, and this is slightly better for White.}) (13... Bf6 {Looks logical, but it's inaccurate.} 14. Qb5 c5 15. Ne4 {White is better here.} Bxb2 $2 {Just loses.} 16. Rb1 $18 {c5 is weak, and Black simply has too many problems.}) 14. Ne4 $132 {It's not an advantage, but we still have an extra pawn, and it's an interesting position. The king on d1 is not weak at all. For example, a game could continue with:} Bxb3 {Is a serious and concrete try.} (14... Bf8 {Also logical.} 15. Qh5 {We have Ng5 ideas, so it makes sense for Black to play:} h6 16. Ne5 $132 {[%cal Ge1e3] With an extra move we would be better with Re3. It's equal with a lot of moves here, but it's definitely playable, and we still keep an extra pawn.}) 15. cxb3 Nxd3 16. Qf5 Nxe1 17. Nxe1 {Here Black is definitely risking more than White.} c4 {Is the best for the engine.} (17... Qd4 {It looks logical to play actively, but here White could be slightly better after:} 18. Kc1 {[%cal Ge1f3]} g6 {The only move, otherwise Black is lost.} 19. Qf3 Rad8 20. Be3 {Even better than Bc3, as it keeps the c3-square for the knight.} Qb4 21. Nc3 Qxb3 22. Rxa7 $14 {And this is already slightly better for White.}) 18. bxc4 Qd4 19. Nc2 Qxb2 20. Qb5 Qxb5 21. cxb5 $132 {We have a playable and interesting endgame that is not at all worse (+0.10 evaluation).} *

