{[%evp 0,56,16,38,58,38,38,-1,12,-11,22,18,13,11,20,9,15,8,6,6,78,57,84,80,80,45,58,47,43,43,43,21,49,41,48,24,44,37,37,35,65,24,22,20,26,0,28,21,19,-19,-8,-26,-13,0,-8,-8,9,0,64]} 1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bb4 5. Nge2 {More interesting than Nf3, and here Black has only one critical move:} (5. Nf3 {Also possible, but if Black plays accurately, it's less interesting.} d5 (5... d6 {Is also perfectly fine for Black.} 6. O-O (6. h3 {Preferable, but also after:} Be6 $11 {Black doesn't seem to have any problems.}) 6... Na5 $11 {Is even an option for Black now.}) 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O {The most interesting way to play for White.} (7. Bd2 {The mainline, but harmless for Black.} Nb6 8. Bb3 O-O 9. h3 {To stop Ng4, but Black is just fine. For example:} (9. O-O {Allows:} Bg4 $132 {And this is also fine for Black.}) 9... Qe7 10. O-O Be6 $11 {With an equal position and no problems for Black.}) 7... Bxc3 8. bxc3 Bg4 {This stops the Qe1 idea.} (8... O-O {Could lead to an interesting game after:} 9. Qe1 Re8 10. Ng5 {With the idea of Qe4.} Bf5 11. Rb1 h6 12. Rb5 $132 {And here it could be very interesting to play for White.}) (8... Nxc3 $2 {Simply loses.} 9. Qe1 $18 {With Nxe5 and Ba3 ideas, Black is busted.}) 9. Re1 (9. Qe1 $2 {Is simply bad here.} Bxf3 $17 {With a big advantage for Black.}) 9... O-O $11 {Black just forced us to play Re1, which is less interesting than Qe1.}) 5... d5 (5... d6 {The second most common.} 6. O-O {O-O transposes into the 5...O-O line. So let's see some alternatives:} Na5 (6... Be6 {Also seems logical.} 7. Nd5 $16 {But here White is seriously better.}) 7. Nd5 Nxc4 8. Nxb4 Nb6 9. c4 $16 {With a big advantage for White.}) (5... Na5 $2 {Is just bad.} 6. a3 Bxc3+ 7. Nxc3 Nxc4 8. dxc4 {Here White actually has a serious advantage. For example:} h6 9. Be3 d6 10. f3 Be6 11. Qe2 Qe7 12. O-O-O a6 {Ivanchuk - Giri, 2011.} 13. Kb1 {An improvement over Ivanchuk's b3.} O-O-O 14. Nd5 $16 {And White simply has a very comfortable advantage.}) (5... O-O {Just gives us a great game.} 6. O-O d6 7. f4 $14 {And White definitely has some advantage.}) 6. exd5 Nxd5 7. O-O {The mainline, and here Black has four moves.} Be6 {The most common and challenging line.} (7... Nxc3 {Second most common move.} 8. bxc3 Bd6 (8... Be7 {Also possible, and has recently been played in Nakamura - Sevian, 2024.} 9. a4 {Just to make sure that our bishop has enough squares.} O-O 10. a5 $132 {Other moves are also good, and we have a more pleasant game. But objectively it's equal.} (10. Ng3 {Here Black is in time for:} Kh8 $132 {[%cal Gf7f5] With f5 ideas, which is why we don't start with Ng3.}) 10... Kh8 {This was good against Ng3, so let's see what happens here:} 11. f4 {We clearly see why it was good to stay flexible with the bishop.} f5 12. Kh1 Bf6 {Could be one idea behind Be7, but simply:} 13. Ba3 Be7 14. Qc1 $14 {And the position is interesting for White, even slightly better.}) 9. Ng3 (9. f4 $6 {The second most common, but here I don't believe in this plan.} O-O 10. f5 {Typical, but Black is in time for:} (10. a4 $15 {We are just one move short to be ready to play f5 without losing the bishop. But it makes a big difference, so Black can for example play Be6 now.}) 10... Na5 $15 {[%csl Rc4] And we lose the bishop.}) 9... O-O (9... Na5 {We can just keep the bishop with:} 10. Bd5 c6 11. Be4 O-O {f5 could be Black's next move, but we can play:} 12. Bf5 $14 {With a slightly better position.}) 10. a4 {[%cal Gc4a2] To make sure that we keep the bishop.} (10. Qh5 {Most common, but here Black will take the bishop:} Na5 11. Bd5 c6 12. Be4 g6 $15 {[%cal Gf7f5] Comes with tempo here, and f5 is unstoppable.}) 10... Kh8 11. Ne4 $146 {Hasn't been played, but is very natural and good.} f5 12. Nxd6 cxd6 13. f4 $132 {White doesn't have an advantage, but with the pair of bishops it could be slightly more pleasant to play.}) (7... Nb6 {Black's least common option, immediately going after the bishop.} 8. Bb3 Na5 (8... O-O {Doesn't make much sense now.} 9. a3 Bd6 10. Ba2 {[%cal Gf2f4] We keep the bishop and can go for f4 next.} Re8 (10... Bg4 {Allows us to advance on the kingside:} 11. h3 Bh5 12. g4 Bg6 13. f4 $14 {And White definitely has some advantage here.}) 11. f4 exf4 12. Nxf4 Bxf4 13. Bxf4 Be6 {Black's best option, but it's always more comfortable for White.} 14. Bxe6 Rxe6 15. Qf3 Nd4 16. Qf2 $132 {White has the open f-file, Ne4 ideas and the bishop. So this is definitely pleasant to play.}) 9. f4 Nxb3 {Black seizes the pair of bishops.} 10. axb3 exf4 11. Nxf4 O-O 12. Qf3 {This is definitely more comfortable for White. For example:} c6 {So - Shen, 2007.} 13. Be3 {[%cal Gf3f2,Ge3d4,Gf4h5] So immediately played Nh5, but I think that we can save the idea for later. It's unclear for Black how to play.} Nd7 14. Qf2 Nf6 15. h3 b6 {Just don't take on b6, as it would be a blunder.} 16. Nfe2 $36 {[%cal Ga1a4,Ga4f4] White can bring the rook into the game via a4 for example, and it's just more pleasant to play.}) (7... Bxc3 {Black's least convincing try.} 8. Nxc3 Nxc3 9. bxc3 Na5 {The only justification for giving up the pair of bishops.} (9... O-O {After giving up the pair of bishops, this doesn't make sense.} 10. Qf3 {Here Be6 is necessary, but sad.} Na5 $2 {Just loses to:} 11. Ba3 $18 {Re8 is not possible, as f7 would be hanging.}) 10. Bb3 {White's simplest continuation.} (10. Bb5+ {It's also possible to keep the pair of bishops.} c6 11. Ba4 O-O 12. c4 $36 {Bd2 next for example, and White definitely achieved something.}) 10... Nxb3 11. axb3 O-O 12. f4 exf4 13. Bxf4 $36 {It's close to objective equality, but over the board Black feels the pressure and White scored 75% from this position.}) 8. Bxd5 Bxd5 9. f4 {Most interesting move.} (9. a3 {White can regain the bishop, but this doesn't pose problems for Black.} Bxc3 10. Nxc3 O-O 11. f4 f6 $11 {Nakamura - Vidit, 2022. I don't think that this is interesting for White.}) 9... f6 $1 {Only played in 51 out of 236 games, but this is the toughest move for us.} (9... O-O {Is the most common move, but this definitely gives us chances.} 10. Nxd5 {We need to make sure that Black plays with the dark-squared bishop.} (10. f5 {Most players started with this, but it allows:} Bxc3 11. Nxc3 f6 $11 {And now Black is just absolutely fine.}) 10... Qxd5 11. f5 {[%csl Be4,Bf5][%cal Ge2g3,Gg3e4,Gd1g4] We have more space on the kingside and a very nice square on e4 for the knight. It's not an advantage, but White is more comfortable.} f6 (11... e4 {A concrete attempt.} 12. d4 $14 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gc2c3,Gd1b3] But here White already was slightly better in Alinasab - Lobanov, 2023.}) 12. Ng3 Bc5+ {Most common move, and what the engine prefers.} (12... Rf7 {Has been tried in three games.} 13. Ne4 Bf8 14. Be3 Nd4 15. c3 {Has been played by Petrov here.} (15. g4 $14 {[%cal Gf1f2,Gd1f1,Gh2h4] It's also fine to play without c3, as there is no need for it.}) 15... Nc6 16. Qe2 Rd8 17. Rad1 Qxa2 18. g4 $14 {Petrov - Dragomarezkij, 2022. With an attack, and White won this game.}) 13. Kh1 Rad8 14. Ne4 Bb6 15. b3 $132 {And it's always more pleasant for White, although it's objectively equal.} (15. Bd2 $132 {We can also play with g4 ideas in the future.})) (9... Bxc3 {Rare, but approved by the engine.} 10. Nxc3 O-O 11. Qg4 $132 {This is definitely playable for White.}) 10. fxe5 Nxe5 11. Nxd5 (11. Nf4 $2 {Not interesting at all, Black just plays:} Bf7 $15 {And it's even worse for White.}) 11... Qxd5 12. d4 {Played in six games, and here I have a small idea for White.} (12. c3 {Most common, but easy for Black to meet.} Bd6 13. d4 (13. Nf4 {Is what was usually played, now here it's not the best.} Qf7 14. d4 O-O-O $132 {This is just good for Black, as the knight is not hanging at all.} 15. Qa4 a6 16. dxe5 {And here Black's point is:} Bc5+ 17. Kh1 fxe5 $15 {White cannot move the knight due to the hanging rook.}) 13... Ng6 14. Nf4 {Black can just trade everything on f4.} Nxf4 15. Bxf4 Bxf4 16. Rxf4 O-O-O $11 {And this is just nothing for White.}) 12... Ng6 $1 {Most precise for Black.} (12... O-O-O {Was played in the other three games, but it gives us some chances.} 13. c3 Bd6 (13... Ba5 {Matta - Pranesh, 2025. Here I think that White definitely gets something interesting.} 14. Nf4 Qd7 15. b4 Bb6 16. a4 a6 17. Kh1 $14 {Rhe8 is the only move for Black to not be seriously worse, and we can maneuver the knight to f5 for example, and this could be slightly better.}) 14. Bf4 {To trade bishops.} Ng6 15. Bxd6 Qxd6 16. Ng3 $36 {Nf5 cannot be stopped, as Ne7 fails to Qg4+, and with the knights remaining I think that White can play for an advantage.}) 13. Nf4 Nxf4 14. Bxf4 O-O-O 15. Qd3 {[%cal Ga2a3,Gc2c4] My idea here is to play for c4. Of course Black equalizes with precise play, but that's the situation with theory nowadays. The e4-e5 setup is one of the soundest, if not the soundest openings for Black, so we can only play for some small chances.} (15. c3 {Offers no chances.} Bd6 $11 {This is just very drawish.}) 15... a6 {A waiting move, as c4 is not possible due to Qxd4, and then we don't have c3.} (15... Bd6 {Allows:} 16. c4 Qc6 17. Be3 $132 {It's not an advantage, but this could be playable for White and slightly more pleasant.}) 16. a3 Bd6 {Probably the best for Black.} (16... Be7 {To not allow c4.} 17. Rae1 Qd7 18. c3 {But perhaps we gained a tempo now. And if Black goes for the trade now:} Bd6 19. Bxd6 Qxd6 20. Qf5+ Qd7 21. Re6 {We can try to play for the e-file.} Rhe8 {It makes sense for Black to contest the e-file, but this loses a pawn:} 22. Rxe8 Rxe8 23. Qxh7 Re2 24. Qh8+ Re8 25. Qh5 $11 {This still should be a draw, but Black needs to prove it. So we keep some practical chances.}) 17. c4 {And this could be playable. For example:} Qh5 {Is the best for the engine.} 18. Bxd6 Rxd6 19. Rae1 Re8 {Challenging the e-file is the best way for Black to play.} (19... Rhd8 {Looks logical, but after:} 20. d5 $36 {White could be slightly better.}) 20. d5 Rdd8 21. h3 {Black cannot exchange all of the rooks now, as h7 would be hanging. So it makes sense to prepare it with:} h6 {And it's very close to a draw. We can maybe try:} 22. Qd4 Rxe1 23. Rxe1 Re8 24. Qg4+ {Forcing a king's endgame.} Qxg4 25. Rxe8+ Kd7 26. hxg4 Kxe8 27. b4 {In the beginning the engine was jumping a little, but after some time it shows a draw with:} Ke7 28. c5 f5 $11 {And now this is fully equal.} *

