} Bb6 (7... Bb4+ 8. c3 Ba5 9. b4 Bb6 10. Nxb6 axb6 11. O-O $16 {[%cal Gf4f5];0-1 Mekhitarian,K (2554)-Esipenko,A (2682) Speed Chess Super Swiss Chess.com INT blitz 2020 (15)}) (7... exf4 8. Nxc5 dxc5 9. Bxf4 $14 {[%cal Ge1g1]}) 8. Nxb6 axb6 9. O-O $14 {[%cal Gf4f5,Gf4e5]}) (6... Na5 $5 7. Bb3 Nxb3 8. axb3 a6 {[%cal Gc5a7] Securing the bishop.} (8... O-O 9. Na4 $1 $14) 9. Qe2 Bg4 10. Be3 $14) (6... a6 7. Nd5 $1 (7. f5 $6 b5 8. Bb3 Na5 $36 {[%csl Rb3][%cal Gc8b7,Gd6d5];1-0 Harsha,B (2569)-Narayanan,S (2361) Biel HTO 57th 2024 (2)}) 7... Nxd5 (7... Bg4 8. c3 $1 {[%cal Rc6d4] Stopping Nd4.} Ba7 {Probably the best, but we have a nice game after} (8... O-O 9. f5 $16 {[%cal Gc1g5,Gh2h3]}) 9. f5 $36 {[%cal Gc1g5]}) (7... h6 8. f5 Na5 (8... Ba7 9. Bd2 $1 $36 {[%cal Rc6a5,Gd1e2,Ge1c1] We stop Na5, and can continue with Qe2-O-O-O.}) 9. b4 $1 {Key move.} Nxd5 10. bxc5 Nf6 11. Bb3 (11. Bd5 $5 $14 {Also deserves attention.}) 11... dxc5 12. Nxe5 Nxb3 13. axb3 {Now Black must find the only move} Bxf5 $1 (13... Qd4 $2 14. Bf4 $18) 14. Bb2 $1 {[%cal Rd8d4]} (14. exf5 Qd4 $17 {[%cal Rc1f4]}) 14... Be6 15. O-O $36 {[%csl Bb2][%cal Gb2h8]}) 8. Bxd5 Be6 $1 {Black's best.} (8... O-O $2 9. f5 $1 $16 {[%cal Gc1g5];1-0 Sokolov,A (2380)-Karpatchev,A (2570) RUS-Cup06 Nizhnij Novgorod 1998 (1)}) (8... exf4 9. d4 $1 Bb6 (9... Nxd4 10. Nxd4 Qh4+ 11. Kf1 $14 {1-0 So,W-Sales,J (2340) PHI-ch Tagaytay City 2004 (2)}) 10. Bxf4 Be6 11. O-O O-O 12. Kh1 $14 {½-½ Wei,Y (2634)-Vallejo Pons,F (2698) Leon 27th rapid 2014 (2.2)}) (8... Qe7 9. f5 $1 $36) 9. Bxc6+ $5 bxc6 10. fxe5 dxe5 11. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3] Be3 next. The position is equal, but very playable.}) (6... a5 $5 7. f5 {[%csl Bf5][%cal Rb7b5] Compared to the 6...a6 line, here Black doesn't have the b5 idea.} (7. Nd5 $2 Nxd5 8. Bxd5 exf4 9. d4 Nxd4 $1 10. Nxd4 Qh4+ 11. Kf1 f3 $1 12. gxf3 c6 13. Bb3 {[%csl Ba5] White is much better when Black's pawn is on a6, but here} a4 $1 {[%csl Ba4]} 14. Bc4 b5 $19 {[%csl Rc4]}) 7... Nd4 8. a4 $1 {[%cal Rb7b5]} 