1. e4 e5 2. Nc3 Nc6 3. Bc4 Nf6 4. d3 Bc5 {[%csl Bc5] The bishop on c5 aims to transpose into the mainstream Italian.} 5. f4 O-O 6. Nf3 d6 (6... Ng4 {[%csl Bg4] A serious but rare line.} 7. Qe2 $1 {[%cal Rg4f2] A prophylactic move against Nf2.} (7. Ng5 d5 $1 8. Nxd5 exf4 9. Bxf4 h6 $13 {1-0 Hong,A (2504)-Liang,A (2613) Junior Speed Qual Chess.com INT blitz 2022 (1.39)}) 7... Bf2+ $1 {The best move, but Black should be well-prepared to play it.} (7... Nf2 $2 8. Rf1 Ng4 9. f5 $18) (7... exf4 8. Bxf4 d6 (8... Bf2+ $6 9. Kd2 Bb6 10. Bg5 Qe8 11. Nd5 $16) 9. Bg5 Nd4 $1 (9... Qd7 10. O-O-O Nf2 11. d4 $1 Nxd1 12. Rxd1 Bb6 13. Nd5 $16 {It's not only compensation but an advantage.}) 10. Bxd8 Nxe2 {Now we can choose between two playable endgames:} 11. Bxc7 (11. Kxe2 Rxd8 12. Na4 $14) 11... Nxc3 12. bxc3 Be6 $1 13. Bxe6 fxe6 14. d4 Ba3 15. Rb1 b6 16. Rb3 Rf7 17. Rxa3 Rxc7 18. Kd2 $14) (7... d6 $2 {The second most common move, but a positional mistake.} 8. f5 $18 {[%cal Gc1g5] Bg5 next, and our position is already winning.}) (7... Nd4 8. Nxd4 exd4 $1 (8... Bxd4 $2 9. f5 $16) 9. Nd5 Nh6 $1 (9... d6 10. b4 $1 $16) 10. f5 (10. b4 $2 Bd6 $17) 10... c6 $1 {The only move.} 11. O-O (11. f6 $5 cxd5 12. O-O dxc4 13. Qh5 Ng4 $1 14. Qxg4 g6 15. Qg5 Re8 {[%cal Gc5f8]} 16. Qxc5 d5 $1 $11) 11... cxd5 12. Bxd5 {[%cal Gf5f6] f6 is a strong threat.} Be7 $1 {The only move against f6.} 13. e5 {[%cal Gf5f6]} (13. Qh5 $5 {[%cal Re7g5] against Bg5.} Kh8 $1 14. f6 Bxf6 15. e5 Ng8 $1 16. exf6 Nxf6 17. Qf3 Nxd5 18. Qxd5 $36) 13... Bg5 {Necessary.} 14. Qh5 Bxc1 15. Raxc1 {Black holds with only moves, but the final position is still interesting for us:} d6 16. f6 Bf5 $1 {[%csl Bf5]} 17. Rxf5 Nxf5 18. Qxf5 dxe5 19. fxg7 Kxg7 20. Rf1 {[%cal Rd8d5,Gf5f6] Using the fact that Qxd5 is impossible due to Qf6+.} Qe7 (20... Qxd5 $4 21. Qg5+ Kh8 22. Qf6+ Kg8 23. Rf5 $18 {[%cal Gf5g5]}) 21. Bxb7 $5 Rab8 22. Be4 Rh8 23. b3 $36 {[%csl Be4]}) 8. Kf1 Nd4 (8... Bb6 $2 9. h3 $16) 9. Nxd4 Bxd4 10. f5 (10. Qxg4 d5 $1 {is Black's idea.} 11. f5 dxc4 12. Nd5 $13 {Balanced and complicated.}) (10. h3 $5 {deserves attention if you're interested in the arising complications.}) 10... Nf2 $1 {The best move, but there are no games here.} (10... Qh4 $2 11. h3 Nf6 12. Qf3 $16 {[%csl Rh4][%cal Gg2g3] g3 next. The queen on h4 is misplaced.}) (10... Nf6 $6 11. g4 $36 {[%cal Gg4g5] A strong attack is coming. The king on f1 is not a problem.} (11. Bg5 {0-1 Yeletsky,I (2498)-Grigorjev,I (2433) Titled Tuesday intern op 04th Jun Late Chess.com INT blitz 2024 (5)})) 11. Qh5 {[%cal Gc1g5] Preparing Bg5.} c6 $1 {[%cal Gd8b6] An important only move: Black frees the b6-square for the queen.} (11... Nxh1 $4 12. Bg5 Qe8 13. Nd5 $18) 12. Bg5 Qb6 $1 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Gb6b2] Counterplay.} (12... Qa5 $2 13. Be7 $18) 13. Na4 Qa5 14. c3 Qxa4 15. cxd4 Nxh1 16. f6 (16. Be7 $5 Qc2 $1 (16... d5 17. f6 Qc2 $1 {[%csl Bc2] The only move, leading to the same position.}) 17. Kg1 d5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] The only move.} 18. f6 Qd2 $1 {[%cal Rh5g5,Gd2g5] Holding the g5-square.} (18... Qxb2 $2 19. Rf1 $18) 19. exd5 cxd5 20. Bxd5 Qxb2 21. Rd1 $13 {The evaluation is 0.00 but very complicated, so it's not just a boring draw.} (21. Rf1 $2 Qxd4+ 22. Kxh1 Qxd3 $15 {[%csl Rf1]})) 16... d5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] The only move. Once again, we have many tries.} 17. Bh4 (17. Be3 $5 gxf6 $1 18. Qh6 $13) (17. Bd2 gxf6 18. dxe5 Qc2 $1 {[%csl Bc2]} 19. Qh6 fxe5 20. Qg5+ $11 {[%cal Gg5f6,Gf6g5] Perpetual check follows.}) 17... h6 $3 {[%cal Rh5g5] The only move, preventing Qg5.} 18. fxg7 Kxg7 19. Kg1 dxc4 20. Bg5 hxg5 21. Qxg5+ $11 {[%cal Gg5h5,Gh5g5]}) 7. f5 $1 {[%cal Cd6d5,Gg2g4,Gc1g5] We block the kingside and are ready to create a quick attack with Bg5-g4. Black's main counterplay plan is d6-d5, which must be prepared.} Nd4 $1 {[%csl Bd4][%cal Gc7c6,Gd6d5] Black's best reaction, with the idea of c6-c5.} (7... Ng4 $2 8. Bg5 $18 {[%cal Rf7f6];0-1 Curtis,J (2292)-Travesset Sagre,J (2315) Titled Tuesday intern op 22nd Oct Late Chess.com INT blitz 2024 (10) The f6 move is not possible.}) (7... a6 $2 {[%cal Gc5a7] Black secures the a7-square for the bishop but loses an important tempo.} 8. Bg5 $1 {[%csl Gg5][%cal Gc3d5] Nd5 is a huge threat.} Nb8 {[%cal Gb8d7] A logical move to defend the f6-knight.} 9. Nd5 Nbd7 10. a3 c6 11. Nxf6+ Nxf6 12. Qe2 $18 {[%cal Ge1c1,Gg2g4] O-O-O followed by g2-g4, and according to the engine, White's position is already winning.}) (7... h6 {[%csl Rh6][%cal Rc1g5,Gg2g4,Gg4g5] Black stops Bg5, but our future g4-g5 plan will be stronger.} 8. a3 {[%csl Ba3,Yc4][%cal Yc4a2] Securing a square for the bishop. I cannot see too many plans for Black.} (8. Nd5 $5 {Also fine.} Nxd5 9. Bxd5 Ne7 10. Bb3 d5 11. f6 $1 Ng6 12. fxg7 Kxg7 13. Bxd5 $36) (8. Qe2 a6 9. g4 Nxg4 10. Qg2 Nf6 11. Bxh6 Nh5 12. Bg5 Nf6 13. Nd5 Nxd5 14. Bxd5 Qe8 15. Bf6 {1-0 Amar,E (2518)-Dehtiarov,R (2300) EU-ch Blitz Katowice 2022 (5)}) 8... Ne7 {[%cal Gd6d5] Most logical, preparing d5.} 9. Qe2 d5 10. Ba2 $40 {[%csl Rg8][%cal Gg2g4] g4 next, with a huge attack.}) (7... Na5 {Black's most common continuation, but inaccurate!} 8. Bg5 $1 Nxc4 {This looks less logical than 8...c6 9.a3 Nxc4, but it's Black's best continuation due to a concrete idea.} (8... c6 {[%cal Rc3d5] Against Nd5 and with the idea of d6-d5.} 9. a3 $1 Nxc4 (9... d5 10. Ba2 $18 {[%cal Gb2b4]}) 10. dxc4 $14 {[%cal Gd1d3,Ge1c1] Qd3-O-O-O next, with a huge advantage.}) 9. dxc4 b5 $1 {An engine improvement. White will be better here too, but at least Black has counterplay.} (9... c6 $2 10. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Ge1c1]}) (9... Bb4 10. Bxf6 Bxc3+ 11. bxc3 Qxf6 12. g4 Qh6 13. g5 Qh5 14. Qd3 Bxf5 15. exf5 e4 16. Qe3 exf3 17. O-O-O Rfe8 18. Qf4 Re5 19. h4 Rae8 20. c5 Re4 21. cxd6 Rxf4 22. d7 Rd8 23. Rhe1 Kf8 {0-1 Beerdsen,T (2510)-Murzin,V (2658) Titled Tuesday intern op 01st Apr Early Chess.com INT blitz 2025 (7)}) 10. cxb5 (10. h4 $5 $40 {[%cal Gf3h2,Gh2g4] Nh2-Ng4 next is also a strong idea.}) 10... Bb7 11. Qd3 Bb4 $1 {[%csl Re4] Counterplay against e4.} 12. Nd2 a6 $1 13. bxa6 Rxa6 14. O-O $14 {Black has compensation, but not full.}) 8. Nd5 $1 {[%csl Bd5] Important to play this now, before Black has the chance to play c7-c6.} (8. Bg5 $2 {The most common and logical move, but here it's a mistake.} c6 $15 {Black is better, so be careful.}) 8... Nxd5 9. Bxd5 c6 10. Bb3 {[%cal Gf3d4,Gd1h5] We are ready to continue with Nxd4, followed by Qh5.} (10. Nxd4 cxd5 11. Nf3 dxe4 12. dxe4 Bd7 13. Qd3 Qb6 14. Nd2 Bf2+ 15. Ke2 d5 16. exd5 e4 17. Qb3 Bxf5 18. Qxb6 Bxb6 19. Nc4 Bg4+ 20. Ke1 Rac8 21. Nxb6 axb6 22. c3 Rfd8 23. h3 Bh5 24. g4 Bg6 25. Be3 Rxd5 26. Rh2 b5 27. Rd1 Rd3 28. Rxd3 exd3 29. Rf2 Be4 30. Kd2 f6 31. Rf4 Re8 32. h4 Re6 33. Bd4 Kf7 34. b3 g6 35. Bxf6 Ke8 36. Bd4 Ke7 37. Bc5+ Kd7 38. g5 Bf5 39. Be3 Ra6 40. Rd4+ Kc6 41. a4 {½-½ Adams,W-Steiner,H Pan American-ch01 Hollywood 1945}) 10... Nxb3 {Black should exchange our bishop.} (10... d5 11. Nxd4 Bxd4 12. Qf3 $36) 11. axb3 d5 12. Qe2 $132 {[%cal Gc1e3,Yg2g4,Yg4g5]} *

